  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how Antarctic krill use sea ice and the water column across winter into late winter or early spring. Near the surface, drifting ice floes carry patchy algae biomass on their undersides, creating a shallow “sea-ice refuge” and food source. Orange arrows indicate both ice drift and ocean currents, which appear to transport larval krill beneath the ice. During the night, the larvae are shown concentrated in the upper tens of meters, closely associated with the ice and algae patches, suggesting feeding and shelter in this near-surface habitat.\n\nAs the season progresses toward sunrise and spring, the figure emphasizes increasing physical forcing and melting processes, shown by downward green arrows from the ice. These processes seem to disrupt or alter the under-ice habitat. At the same time, krill are also shown much deeper in the water column, including around several hundred meters and near the seafloor, indicating that not all individuals remain under the ice. Overall, the image conveys a seasonal, vertically structured habitat: larvae depend on drifting, algae-bearing sea ice in winter, while broader ocean mixing, melting, and deeper distribution become more important later in the season.",
